::MCR_U02_L07_GuidedPractice_Q01::
Question ID: MCR_U02_L07_GuidedPractice_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.AR.7
Question:
For item 1, A gym membership has a starting fee and a monthly charge. Which response best applies algebraic relationships, equations, inequalities, systems, and piecewise models for the lesson topic: 
{
~A. Choose the largest number without checking what it represents. #This is not the best answer. It skips a required modeling, calculation, unit, or interpretation step needed to meet the lesson standard.
~B. Ignore units and compare values that measure different things. #This is not the best answer. It skips a required modeling, calculation, unit, or interpretation step needed to meet the lesson standard.
~C. Apply a formula without defining the variable or context. #This is not the best answer. It skips a required modeling, calculation, unit, or interpretation step needed to meet the lesson standard.
=D. Define the variable and write an equation or inequality that matches the context. #Correct. This answer matches the mathematical relationship, uses the lesson concept in context, and explains why the result is reasonable.
}

::MCR_U02_L07_GuidedPractice_Q02::
Question ID: MCR_U02_L07_GuidedPractice_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.AR.7
Question:
In item 2, A delivery service charges different rates after a threshold. What should a student do to solve or interpret the situation using 
{
=A. Check the solution by substituting it back into the original situation. #Correct. This answer matches the mathematical relationship, uses the lesson concept in context, and explains why the result is reasonable.
~B. Ignore units and compare values that measure different things. #This is not the best answer. It skips a required modeling, calculation, unit, or interpretation step needed to meet the lesson standard.
~C. Apply a formula without defining the variable or context. #This is not the best answer. It skips a required modeling, calculation, unit, or interpretation step needed to meet the lesson standard.
~D. Use only one point or one value when the relationship requires comparison. #This is not the best answer. It skips a required modeling, calculation, unit, or interpretation step needed to meet the lesson standard.
}

::MCR_U02_L07_GuidedPractice_Q03::
Question ID: MCR_U02_L07_GuidedPractice_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.AR.7
Question:
Item 3 is based on Algebraic Modeling and Interpretation. Two savings plans are represented by equations. Which answer gives the most mathematically reasonable decision?
{
~A. Apply a formula without defining the variable or context. #This is not the best answer. It skips a required modeling, calculation, unit, or interpretation step needed to meet the lesson standard.
=B. Choose a system or piecewise rule when the situation has more than one relationship. #Correct. This answer matches the mathematical relationship, uses the lesson concept in context, and explains why the result is reasonable.
~C. Use only one point or one value when the relationship requires comparison. #This is not the best answer. It skips a required modeling, calculation, unit, or interpretation step needed to meet the lesson standard.
~D. Report a number without explaining what it means in the situation. #This is not the best answer. It skips a required modeling, calculation, unit, or interpretation step needed to meet the lesson standard.
}

